WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.090
The Financial Language You Need.

2
00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:07.899
Here's a number: negative eleven million
dollars. That's the net asset position of

3
00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:13.206
a real company that came through our
pipeline. They had revenue — over four

4
00:00:13.206 --> 00:00:18.585
million a year. They had customers.
They'd been in business for nearly fifty

5
00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:23.964
years. On the surface, the deal looked
like a fit. But when the underwriting

6
00:00:23.964 --> 00:00:28.984
team opened the books, the picture
changed. This was a hardware and IoT

7
00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:34.506
company that owned its full supply chain
— proprietary R&D, manufacturing, app

8
00:00:34.506 --> 00:00:39.598
development. All of that infrastructure
had accumulated years of capital

9
00:00:39.598 --> 00:00:45.120
expenditure that crushed their balance
sheet. They owed eleven million dollars

10
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.415
more than everything they owned.

11
00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:53.098
That's the kind of thing that gets caught
in underwriting. And sometimes, even with

12
00:00:53.098 --> 00:00:58.296
great discovery, a company like that can
still make it through — because the

13
00:00:58.296 --> 00:01:03.563
surface-level picture looks fine. But the
more you understand these financial

14
00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:08.554
concepts, the better you'll be at
spotting the obvious nonfits early, and

15
00:01:08.554 --> 00:01:13.890
the more confident you'll feel about the
deals you do push forward. There will

16
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.880
always be edge cases that reach the
underwriting team, and that's exactly

17
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.009
what they're there for. Your job is to
reduce the noise — not eliminate it.

18
00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:28.574
The concepts in this section are
foundational vocabulary. When you

19
00:01:28.574 --> 00:01:33.912
understand these terms, you'll understand
why the underwriting team makes the

20
00:01:33.912 --> 00:01:39.601
decisions they make, and you'll be better
equipped to have financial conversations

21
00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:41.006
with your prospects.

22
00:01:41.006 --> 00:01:44.628
Over the next few minutes, we're going to
cover three sections.

23
00:01:44.628 --> 00:01:50.935
First — the balance sheet. What a company
owns versus what it owes. We'll cover two

24
00:01:50.935 --> 00:01:54.473
concepts: net asset position and current
ratio.

25
00:01:54.473 --> 00:02:00.436
Second — cash flow. How fast money is
moving in and out of the company. We'll

26
00:02:00.436 --> 00:02:02.554
cover burn rate and runway.

27
00:02:02.554 --> 00:02:07.849
Third — Financial statements. The
documents that tell the financial story.

28
00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:12.354
Section one: Net asset position and
ratios.

29
00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:19.836
Net asset position. Think a homeowner
whose home is worth $500K. They owe $300K

30
00:02:19.836 --> 00:02:27.127
on the mortgage. So their net asset
position is $200K. Same thing applies for

31
00:02:27.127 --> 00:02:34.897
a business. Add up everything they own —
cash, inventory, equipment, receivables —

32
00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:42.476
and subtract everything they owe — loans,
payables, credit lines. What's left is

33
00:02:42.476 --> 00:02:50.342
their net asset position. That company at
negative $11M? Their debts exceeded their

34
00:02:50.342 --> 00:02:51.781
assets by $11M.

35
00:02:51.781 --> 00:02:58.139
Current ratio. This is the "can you pay
your short-term bills" number. Take

36
00:02:58.139 --> 00:03:05.013
current assets — which is your cash and
anything you can turn into cash quickly —

37
00:03:05.013 --> 00:03:11.801
and divide by current liabilities, the
bills due in the next 12 months. So let's

38
00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:18.159
say a prospect has $100K in the bank,
$80K in upcoming bills? You get 1.25.

39
00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:24.852
Here's another example. You have $100K in
the bank and $150K in current

40
00:03:24.852 --> 00:03:32.214
liabilities. That's 0.67. This is a not
so great ration. Our underwriting team

41
00:03:32.214 --> 00:03:39.193
has said a weak current ratio is one of
the quickest ways a prospect won't

42
00:03:39.193 --> 00:03:40.914
qualify for float.

43
00:03:40.914 --> 00:03:44.861
Section two: cash flow — burn rate and
runway.

44
00:03:44.861 --> 00:03:51.921
Burn rate is money going out minus money
coming in, every month. A company

45
00:03:51.921 --> 00:03:59.465
spending $200K a month and bringing in
$150K has a $50K monthly burn. That burn

46
00:03:59.465 --> 00:04:04.784
has to come from somewhere — usually
their bank account.

47
00:04:04.784 --> 00:04:10.612
Which brings us to runway. How many
months can the company survive if revenue

48
00:04:10.612 --> 00:04:16.057
stopped tomorrow? Take their bank
balance, divide by their monthly burn.

49
00:04:16.057 --> 00:04:21.118
$600K in the bank with a $50K burn?
That's 12 months of runway. The

50
00:04:21.118 --> 00:04:27.253
underwriting team wants to see 12 months
of cash on hand for extended terms. Less

51
00:04:27.253 --> 00:04:33.388
than that, and the float we're extending
starts to look like a company in need of

52
00:04:33.388 --> 00:04:36.456
a lifeline instead of a tool for growth.

53
00:04:36.456 --> 00:04:42.215
Section three: financial statements —
P&L, balance sheet, and gross margin.

54
00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:48.439
A P&L — profit and loss statement — Think
of this like the a movie. It shows

55
00:04:48.439 --> 00:04:54.663
revenue minus expenses over a period of
time. The underwriting team reads 24

56
00:04:54.663 --> 00:05:01.302
months of P&Ls to see the trajectory. Is
the company getting more profitable each

57
00:05:01.302 --> 00:05:06.364
quarter? Holding steady? Going under? The
P&L tells the story.

58
00:05:06.364 --> 00:05:12.215
A balance sheet is the photograph. A
single snapshot — what you own and what

59
00:05:12.215 --> 00:05:18.222
you owe on one specific date. The P&L
shows direction. The balance sheet shows

60
00:05:18.222 --> 00:05:20.250
where you stand right now.

61
00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:25.671
The underwriting team needs both a
balance sheet and Profit and loss

62
00:05:25.671 --> 00:05:31.982
statements only when a prospect wants a
card with extended repayment terms (aka

63
00:05:31.982 --> 00:05:38.132
Terms like Net 15 or Net 30). These types
of deals require 3 business days to

64
00:05:38.132 --> 00:05:44.443
review and may require your client to
take a call with the underwriting team to

65
00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:51.078
learn more about the business's goals and
operating modle. More on this in our next

66
00:05:51.078 --> 00:05:51.644
module.

67
00:05:51.644 --> 00:05:58.711
Gross margin. The percentage of revenue
left after you subtract the direct cost

68
00:05:58.711 --> 00:06:05.598
of what you sell. A DTC skincare brand
buying product for $10 and selling for

69
00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:12.665
$50 has an 80% gross margin. A logistics
company moving freight at thin spreads

70
00:06:12.665 --> 00:06:20.005
might run 10 to 30%. Higher margin means
more room for error — more room for us to

71
00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:27.344
extend credit. A company with 70% margins
can absorb a bad month. A company at 12%

72
00:06:27.344 --> 00:06:28.703
has no cushion.

73
00:06:28.703 --> 00:06:34.508
Here's the takeaway for the seller: You
are not being asked to read a balance

74
00:06:34.508 --> 00:06:40.159
sheet like an accountant. But it's
important to play close attention to the

75
00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:45.658
next module so you too can identify great
candidates for underwriting and

76
00:06:45.658 --> 00:06:47.186
ultimately, Dash-fi.

77
00:06:47.186 --> 00:06:52.372
Next — how the underwriting team uses
these concepts to make decisions, and

78
00:06:52.372 --> 00:06:54.895
what a good-fit prospect looks like.
